<title>A discrete SIR infectious disease model</title>
<p><ref>Video 1</ref> | <ref>Video 2</ref> | <ref>Summary</ref> | <ref>Transcripts</ref></p>

<aside><title>Transcript: A discrete SIR infectious disease model, part 1</title>
<p>The SIR model is a model of an infectious disease with three different stages: we have people that are first <em>susceptible</em>, and they become <em>infective</em>, (which means that they can infect other people), and then lastly they become <em>removed</em> (when they get over the disease and they can’t get the disease or die or whatever it is). This model has three state variables, <m>S</m>, for the number of susceptibles, <m>I</m> for the number of infectives, and <m>R</m> for the number of removed. And then (*there are) the transitions; the rates of transition. It turns out that a susceptible person, (susceptible people) get infected at a rate proportional to <m>S</m> times <m>I</m>, with a proportionality constant <m>b</m>, which is the infection rate. And, infectives recover at a rate proportional to <m>I</m> – so it's <m>a</m> times <m>I</m> – with recovery rate <m>a</m>. To write down a discrete dynamical system model for this infectious disease, let's define some notation. Let <m>t</m> equal time in days, and we’ll assume that we’re measuring the populations on days, so those are our snapshots. And so our state variables can be written as <m>S_t</m> (*which equals the number of susceptible in day <m>t</m>), and <m>I_t</m> is the number of infectives in day <m>t</m>, and lastly <m>R_t</m> is the number of removed in day <m>t</m>. Now, we need to interpret these rates. Let’s look at them this way – let’s view these rates (since we have a discrete dynamical system), as just being changes. So let’s let <m>b</m> times <m>S_t</m> times <m>I_t</m> over here -- let that be the number of individuals that are infected in day <m>t</m>.  Similarly, we will let <m>a</m> times <m>I_t</m> be the number of infectives that get better (or get removed I guess) in time <m>t</m> (in day <m>t</m>). These quantities are changes in the state variables, so we can use those to write down our dynamical system. The change in the number of susceptibles in day <m>t</m> to day <m>(t+1)</m> is just the new value <m>S_{t+1}</m> minus the old value, <m>S_t</m>. What should this be equal to? Well, the way that <m>S_t</m> changes is if somehow susceptibles get infected (they are no longer *susceptible.) And this happens on day <m>t</m> (...it happens). Well this is the number that get infected: its <m>b S_t I_t</m>; but of course this reduces the number of *susceptibles, so we need *this change to be negative. (The change in susceptibles is negative, because fewer people are susceptible so we have to put a minus sign here.) And our model is that the change in the number of susceptibles on day <m>t</m> is just <m>b</m> times <m>S_t</m> times <m>I_t</m> with a negative sign. How about the change in the number of infectives? Well, that's going to be <m>I_{t +1}</m> minus <m>I_t</m> –  it's the new value minus the old value. Well, when a susceptible gets infected, then the number of infectives increases, so we should put the same quantity here for (the number of)  the change in the number of infectives, <m>b</m> times <m>S_t</m> times <m>I_t</m>. But the <m>I</m> can also change because infectives get removed. And this happens (well, in day <m>t</m>…) this number of infectives get better, so we should subtract off this quantity because this will reduce the number of infectives. So, the change in the number of infectives should be (negative <m>I</m> times…) –  negative a times <m>I_t</m>.  Lastly, the removed – same idea – we have the change in the number of removed <m>R_{t+1} - R_t</m>. Well, the removed… the number of removed will only increase, it will increase at again this rate, (or the number that it will increase in one day will be <m>a</m> times <m>I_t</m>.) So, that (*<m>a I_t</m>) gets added to the right hand side to give us our dynamical system – discrete dynamical system for the infectious disease model with two unknown parameters of course. In order to specify the system, we have to determine what are <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</p>
</aside>

<aside><title>Transcript: A discrete SIR infectious disease model, part 2</title>
<p>Here are the equations that model the spread of an infectious disease by a discrete dynamical system. Let’s specify values for the parameters. We’ll set <m>a</m>, which is the recovery rate parameter, to be <m>0.05</m>, and then we’ll make <m>b</m>, the infection rate parameter, to be (well here we’re going to have to make it pretty small …you’ll see why in a second) <m>0.0001</m>. Then we should start with some initial conditions: so these were the parameters, the initial conditions, let’s start with initial conditions <m>S_0</m> equals <m>10,000</m>, and <m>I_0</m> – let’s go with <m>1,000</m>. So, <m>10,000</m> folks are susceptible, and <m>1,000</m> that are infected, and we don’t need to start with any recovered so let’s let <m>R_0</m> equal <m>0</m>. So, these are our initial conditions. The initial conditions give us the value of the state variables at time <m>0</m>, and then we use these equations here, called difference equations, which give us the change, to calculate the values of the state variables at future times. If <m>t</m> is <m>0</m>, which is what we care about for the first case in order to calculate the values at time <m>1</m> from time <m>0</m>, then, we can write <m>b S_0 I_0</m>. This is the rate in which (... or number of individuals who…) get infected. Well, that’s going to be <m>b</m> (which is <m>0.0001</m>), times <m>S_0</m> (which is <m>10,000</m>), times <m>I_0</m> (which is <m>1,000</m>). And when you calculate it out it turns out that is <m>1,000</m>. So this means <m>1,000</m> new individuals get infected from time <m>0</m> to time <m>1</m>.*Now, <m>a</m> times <m>I_0</m> is the number of infected individuals that get removed, and that is -- well, <m>a</m> is <m>0.05</m> and <m>I_0</m> is <m>1,000</m> -- so that gives us that only <m>50</m> individuals recover in the first time step. Now we can plug these values into the equation for the change, so <m>S_1</m> minus <m>S_0</m> is equal to negative <m>b</m> times <m>S_t</m> times <m>I_t</m> – so it's negative <m>1,000</m>. In other words, well what do we know?…what’s <m>S_0</m>? <m>S_0</m> is <m>10,000</m> right? So, <m>S_1</m> is <m>1000</m> fewer than that, then <m>S_1</m> is therefore just <m>9,000</m>, so we’re left with only <m>9,000</m> susceptible individuals at time <m>1</m>. How about infectives? Well, <m>I_1</m> minus <m>I_0</m> , the change in the number of infectives equal to <m>b</m> times <m>S_t</m> times <m>I_t</m> – that's the number of new infectives (which again is <m>1,000</m>) minus the number that were removed (and that is minus <m>50</m>), so the change is <m>950</m>. And since <m>I_0</m> was <m>1000</m> and we gained <m>950</m>, that means at the end of time step <m>1</m> we have <m>1,950</m> infected folks. Lastly, *the removed – only way folks get removed is if the infective people are no longer infective. So, that's the rate (the number <m>a</m> times <m>I</m>) which is <m>50</m>. We start off with (*<m>R_0 = </m>) <m>0</m> here, so we just have <m>R_1</m> equals <m>50</m>. After one time step, (which is a day, remember, in this model) we have <m>S_1</m>, which is <m>9,000</m> susceptibles, we have <m>I_1</m>, which is <m>1,950</m> infectives, and we have <m>R_1</m>, or <m>50</m> removed. So, that’s what happens after one time step. So, we’ll do it for one more time step. Well, let’s put in  <m>t = 1</m>, *and calculate the number of new folks that get infected. Well, that’s <m>b S_1 I_1</m>, <m>0.0001</m> times…(well what’s <m>S_1</m>? Now it's not <m>10,000</m> anymore, it's just <m>9,000</m> susceptibles) but we have more infectives – <m>1,950</m>. And if you do that calculation, you should get <m>1,755</m> new infectives. How about infectives that recover and who get removed or die (depending on the disease)? Well, that's <m>a</m>, <m>0.05</m>, times the number of infectives, <m>1,950</m>, and that gives us <m>97.5</m>. So, we have "<m>97</m> and a half" individuals that are no longer infective. Again, we’re not going to worry about these fractional people – we’ll just round in the end – but don’t round while you’re doing the calculations. So we’ll assume there are around <m>98</m> or so people who are recovered. If you plug these values into the equations here you can calculate that the number of susceptible in day <m>2</m>, (since it decreases by <m>1,755</m>) <m>S_2</m>, is down to <m>7,245</m>.
</p>

</aside>
  